# name: test/sql/bugs/315_cpp_default_arguments.test
# description: #315 — a C++ default argument makes its whole parameter vanish, and three nodes that declare a parameter strategy return nothing
# group: [bugs]

# A C++ function with a default argument reports a parameter list that is SHORT
# BUT PLAUSIBLE. Nothing in the row says a parameter is missing, so a query that
# counts arity gets a wrong number with no warning.
#
# Measured before this change (release build at 3e41f6e, context := 'native'):
#     void m1(int a, int b = 2)                        -> ['a : int']                 (1 of 2)
#     void m2(int a, int b, int c = 3)                 -> ['a : int', 'b : int']      (2 of 3)
#     void m3(const std::string& s, bool = true, ...)  -> ['s : const std::string&']  (1 of 3)
#     void dv(const int& x = 0)                        -> []                          (0 of 1)
#     void m4(int a)                                   -> ['a : int']                 (right)
#
# Cause. ExtractCppParametersDirect (src/include/cpp_native_extractors.hpp:618)
# dispatches on two node names:
#
#     if (strcmp(child_type, "parameter_declaration") == 0) { ... }
#     else if (strcmp(child_type, "variadic_parameter_declaration") == 0) { ... }
#
# grammars/tree-sitter-cpp/grammar.js:456 names a defaulted parameter
# `optional_parameter_declaration`, which is neither, so the child is skipped and
# nothing is ever pushed for it.
#
#     optional_parameter_declaration: $ => seq(
#       $._declaration_specifiers,
#       field('declarator', optional(choice($._declarator, $.abstract_reference_declarator))),
#       '=',
#       field('default_value', $.expression),
#     )
#
# Three facts come out of that rule, each measured on a real parse:
#   1. `default_value` is a FIELD, so the fix never guesses which child it is.
#   2. the declarator is OPTIONAL — `void h(int = 2)` is a legal parameter with
#      no name, and must still be reported or arity goes wrong the other way.
#   3. the `=` is a direct anonymous child, between declarator and value.
#
# Fact 3 is what makes the type right. #283's rule is that a parameter's type is
# its declaration with the NAME excised. Applied unchanged to `int b = 2` that
# keeps `int ` and ` = 2`, so the type would read `int = 2`. The fix CLAMPS
# DeclaredTypeText's window to the start of the `=` instead of adding a third
# cut, which also keeps `int b[3] = nullptr` reporting `int[3]` rather than
# losing its array bound.
#
# Also deleted in the same change: ExtractParameterDeclaration's branch for
# `default_parameter_declaration`. That name occurs 0 times in node-types.json,
# grammar.json and grammar.js, so the branch has never run. The #220 shape:
# declared, dispatched, unreachable, green.
#
# THREE MORE NODES, admitted to #315 on 2026-11-07. They share one cause: a node
# that declares a parameter strategy goes looking for a child it already is.
# Measured before this change:
#     void k(int a, int b = 2);   function_declarator -> []   (declares FUNCTION_WITH_PARAMS)
#                                 parameter_list      -> []   (declares FUNCTION_WITH_PARAMS)
#     [](int a, int b = 3)        lambda_expression   -> []   (EVERY param, not just defaults)
#     [](int a, int c)            lambda_expression   -> []   (so its loop was dead, not partial)
#
# HOW THIS FILE WAS BUILT, because it matters for how much to trust it.
#
# It was committed RED first (8a6272b). At that point sections 1-6 were
# PREDICTIONS -- they described behaviour that did not exist -- and sections 7
# and 8 were measured against the pre-fix build. Verified red at :70, the first
# arity assertion, and sections 7-8 verified passing on that same build.
#
# Every prediction then turned out exact. They are measurements now, against
# the build at 7353c95.
#
# The file was also shown to DISCRIMINATE, not merely to pass, by planting two
# faults and rebuilding:
#
#   A. the dispatch arm with no type clamp -- `int b = 2` reported the type
#      `int = 2`, `int b[3] = nullptr` reported `int[3] = nullptr`. Failed at
#      :70. `const char* s`, which has no default, was unchanged, which is the
#      control proving the clamp is a no-op without an `=`.
#   B. the name read by scanning children for an identifier instead of reading
#      the `declarator` field -- `int = x` reported `x : int`, and
#      `int& r = global` reported `global : int& r`, the name leaking into the
#      type as well. Failed at :91, the `dv` row. `int b = 2` and section 1's
#      m3 row still PASSED under fault B, because each of those names is a
#      direct identifier child -- which is why sections 2 and 3 exist.
#
# Section 10 pins #324, a defect this fix made visible rather than caused.

require sitting_duck

# =============================================================================
# 1. The fix — every declared parameter is reported
#    Measured before: 1 of 2, 2 of 3, 1 of 3, 0 of 1.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void m1(int a, int b = 2) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['a : int', 'b : int']

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void m2(int a, int b, int c = 3) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['a : int', 'b : int', 'c : int']

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void m3(const std::string& s, bool flag = true, int n = 0) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['s : const std::string&', 'flag : bool', 'n : int']

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void dv(const int& x = 0) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['x : const int&']

# The arity on its own, so a reader sees the number and not only the payload.
# This is the assertion a user's own COUNT query is really asking.
query II
SELECT 3 AS declared, len(parameters) AS reported
FROM parse_ast('void m2(int a, int b, int c = 3) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
3	3

# =============================================================================
# 2. The type is the declaration with the name AND the default removed
#    A type of `int = 2` is what the one-arm fix produces. The window is clamped
#    at the `=` rather than cut from the name, which is why the array bound and
#    the template argument list both survive.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void arr(int a, int b[3] = nullptr) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['a : int', 'b : int[3]']

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void mp(std::map<int,int> m = {}) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['m : std::map<int,int>']

# A default value containing a comma must not split the parameter list, and must
# not reach the type. Measured before: [] — the parameter was dropped entirely.
query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void ce(int a = g(1,2)) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['a : int']

# =============================================================================
# 3. The trap: a bare identifier sitting where a name is absent
#    This is the recurring defect class of v1.18.3 — a parameter's real name is
#    nested, and a plausible WRONG candidate sits next to it. Here there is no
#    name at all, and the default value is a bare identifier.
#
#    `int = x` parses as:  primitive_type `int` | `=` | identifier `x`
#    with NO declarator field. So the name must come back EMPTY. An
#    implementation that scanned children for an identifier instead of reading
#    the `declarator` field would report `x`, and the AST row already does
#    exactly that — see section 8.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void absx(int a, int = x) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['a : int', '<e> : int']

# The same shape with a literal default, which has no identifier to mistake.
query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void ab(int a, int = 2) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['a : int', '<e> : int']

# A reference whose default is an identifier. The native payload must give the
# name `r`; the AST row gives `global`, which is #318 and is pinned in section 8.
query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void refg(int& r = global) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['r : int&']

# =============================================================================
# 4. A prototype — function_declarator declares FUNCTION_WITH_PARAMS
#    Measured before: []. ExtractCppParameters searched for a
#    function_declarator CHILD, so a node that IS one found nothing. A
#    declaration without a body therefore reported no parameters at all,
#    defaulted or not.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void k(int a, int b = 2);', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_declarator';
----
['a : int', 'b : int']

# The same node in a DEFINITION, which is the control proving section 4 is about
# the node type and not about the missing body. Measured before: [] here too,
# while its parent function_definition reported ['a : int'].
query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void d(int a, int b = 2) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_declarator';
----
['a : int', 'b : int']

# =============================================================================
# 5. A lambda — ExtractLambdaParameters was a second, stale copy of the dispatch
#    Measured before: [] for EVERY parameter, defaulted or not, because it
#    looked for parameter_list among the lambda's direct children while the
#    grammar nests it under lambda_declarator.
#
#    The no-default case is the control that proves the loop was DEAD rather
#    than merely missing the optional-parameter arm. Without it, a fix that only
#    added the arm would look correct here.
# =============================================================================

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('auto l = [](int a, int c) { return a; };', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'lambda_expression';
----
['a : int', 'c : int']

query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('auto l = [](int a, int b = 3) { return a; };', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'lambda_expression';
----
['a : int', 'b : int']

# =============================================================================
# 6. A parameter_list on its own — it also declares FUNCTION_WITH_PARAMS
#    (languages/cpp/cpp_types.def:774). Measured before: [].
#    Its signature_type must stay NULL: IsTypeNode is false for every direct
#    child of a parameter_list, so ExtractCppReturnType finds nothing. That half
#    IS measured — NULL today — and must not move.
# =============================================================================

query II
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR,
       coalesce(signature_type, '<null>')
FROM parse_ast('void d(int a, int b = 2) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'parameter_list';
----
['a : int', 'b : int']	<null>

# =============================================================================
# 7. Controls — measured NOW, and none of them may move
#    Every value below was read out of the release build at 3e41f6e before a
#    line of the fix was written.
# =============================================================================

# The issue's own control: a function with no default argument at all.
query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void m4(int a) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['a : int']

# #283's declared-type rule, with no default anywhere. The pointer and the
# reference keep their qualifiers, and all three names resolve through nested
# declarators. If the clamp ever applied where there is no `=`, this moves.
query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('void nd(const char* s, int& r, int a) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['s : const char*', 'r : int&', 'a : int']

# #283's parameter pack. variadic_parameter_declaration has no default_value
# field, so the clamp must be a no-op for it.
query I
SELECT list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('template <typename... Args> void vari(const Args&... args) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
['args : const Args&...']

# The return type of a defaulted-parameter function is untouched by all of this.
query I
SELECT coalesce(signature_type, '<null>')
FROM parse_ast('void m1(int a, int b = 2) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_definition';
----
void

# =============================================================================
# 8. The AST row `name` — #318 landed, so these are now CORRECT
#    This block was written while #315 landed, pinning the row names at their
#    measured WRONG values and stating the post-fix oracle in this comment:
#    "every row below becomes the declarator-id: b, s, p, r, ''". That is now
#    what it asserts, which is the block being REWRITTEN rather than
#    re-recorded, as the comment required.
#
#    What they were before #318, for the record:
#        int b = 2                   b             (was already right)
#        const std::string& s = "x"  std::string   the TYPE
#        int* p = nullptr            <e>
#        int& r = global             global        the DEFAULT VALUE
#        int = 2                     <e>           (was already right)
#
#    #318 repointed cpp_types.def:191 and :196 from FIND_IDENTIFIER to
#    FIND_IN_DECLARATOR, and gave that strategy the declarator walk #283
#    wrote. The native `parameters` payload is unchanged by it, which is what
#    section 7's controls prove.
# =============================================================================

query TT
SELECT peek, coalesce(nullif(name, ''), '<e>')
FROM parse_ast('void f(int b = 2, const std::string& s = "x", int* p = nullptr, int& r = global, int = 2) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'optional_parameter_declaration'
ORDER BY node_id;
----
int b = 2	b
const std::string& s = "x"	s
int* p = nullptr	p
int& r = global	r
int = 2	<e>

# The same forms without a default. Before #318 these were the control that
# made the rows above #318's and not #315's -- adding a default turned a
# MISSING name into a WRONG one, and the missing name was there either way.
# Measured before #318: <e>, <e>, a.
query TT
SELECT peek, coalesce(nullif(name, ''), '<e>')
FROM parse_ast('void g(const char* q, int& r, int a) {}', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'parameter_declaration'
ORDER BY node_id;
----
const char* q	q
int& r	r
int a	a

# =============================================================================
# 9. Where the corpus count lives
#    test/data/** holds ZERO optional_parameter_declaration nodes, which is why
#    every probe above is an inline parse_ast string and no fixture was added
#    (bug 055: an unbounded glob moves when any file is added beside it).
#    Our own src/ tree holds 67 of them — 2 in *.cpp and 65 in *.hpp — so the
#    construct is common in real code and absent from what CI reads.
#
#    is_optional and default_value are set on ParameterInfo by this fix, but
#    neither reaches SQL: `parameters` is STRUCT("name" VARCHAR, "type" VARCHAR)[].
#    So no assertion here covers them, and none pretends to. Exposing them is
#    #255's territory, and #315 does not need it.
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# 10. #324 — pinned at its measured WRONG value
#     Giving function_declarator its parameters (section 4) exposed a defect
#     that an empty list had been hiding. A function returning a FUNCTION
#     POINTER has two nested function_declarator nodes, each with its own
#     `parameters` field, and the OUTER one's field belongs to the returned
#     function type rather than to the declared function.
#
#     So the outer row below reports `<e> : char` -- the parameter of the
#     returned `int(char)` -- where the function's own parameter is `a : int`.
#     The inner row is correct, and is the control that makes this a nesting
#     defect rather than a parameters defect.
#
#     This is NOT an argument against section 4. Before #315 both rows read
#     `[]`, so a wrong answer was hidden behind a missing one; the node
#     declares FUNCTION_WITH_PARAMS at languages/cpp/cpp_types.def:78 and
#     delivered nothing. Fixing #324 means walking the `declarator` field chain
#     to the innermost function_declarator, which is the walk FindDeclaratorId
#     already performs.
#
#     The outer row's empty `name` is #318's, not #324's.
# =============================================================================

query TTT
SELECT peek, coalesce(nullif(name,''),'<e>'),
       list_transform(parameters, lambda p: coalesce(nullif(p.name,''),'<e>') || ' : ' || coalesce(nullif(p.type,''),'-'))::VARCHAR
FROM parse_ast('int (*fp_ret(int a))(char);', 'cpp', context := 'native')
WHERE type = 'function_declarator'
ORDER BY node_id;
----
(*fp_ret(int a))(char)	<e>	['<e> : char']
fp_ret(int a)	fp_ret	['a : int']
